\section{Glossary and Data Details}
\label{app:glossary}

\begin{table}[ht]
\floatconts
  {tab:glossary}
  {\caption{Glossary.}}
  {\small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lL{0.74\linewidth}@{}}
  \toprule
  Term & Meaning \\
  \midrule
  SUS & Unified Health System, Brazil's public health system \\
  CNS & national health-card number; released encrypted \\
  SIA & outpatient information system \\
  \quad BI & individual outpatient bulletins (CNS + birth date) \\
  \quad AQ, AR & chemotherapy and radiotherapy authorisations (CNS + age) \\
  SIH & hospital information system (one row per admission claim) \\
  SIM & mortality information system (one row per death certificate) \\
  RHC & hospital cancer registry of the National Cancer Institute \\
  File pair & the two outpatient sub-files linked blind: BI and AQ \\
  Pipeline & the whole linkage of SUS records: cascade R1--R4, C1 and C2 \\
  Registry & the cancer registry (RHC); the death registry is SIM \\
  R1--R4 & the four rules of the hospital-claim cascade (Table~\ref{tab:keys}) \\
  C1, C2 & death from a flag on the patient's own records; death from SIM by the demographic link \\
  OS; HR & overall survival; hazard ratio \\
  FNR & false-negative rate, $1-$recall \\
  \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\runin{Nephroblastoma cohort} The cancer registry records one row per tumour, and
bilateral disease produces two; the cohort is collapsed to one row per patient
(counting tumour records instead inflates $n$ by 20.6\%).

\runin{Breast-cancer cohort} Patients linked to the cancer registry, stage I--IV,
non-negative follow-up under all three labels and complete covariates
({{C-N}}); administrative end 31 December 2024; split 70/30 stratified by stage
with a fixed seed; no penalisation. Calibration error is the absolute difference
between mean predicted and Kaplan--Meier observed 5-year risk, averaged over
deciles of predicted risk.

\section{National Audit: Definitions and Full Results}
\label{app:audit}

\subsection{CNS Validation and Missingness}
The check-digit implementation agreed with an independent open-source
implementation on 301{,}010 synthetic checks, including 50{,}053 cases of the
branch where the check-digit remainder gives 10 and 1{,}000 single-digit corruptions. Of 3.42 billion records
with a well-formed CNS (about 235.6 million distinct numbers), 99.9999\% pass the
check digit. Missingness is not at random (Table~\ref{tab:mnar}): unreliability
is highest in early years, where race is not recorded, and under one year of age
(37.0\%), when a newborn has often not yet received a personal CNS. Standardised
to the overall distribution of years, CNS unreliability is {{E-YEAR-PRETA}}\%
(crude {{E-YEAR-PRETA-RAW}}\%) for Black, {{E-YEAR-PARDA}}\%
({{E-YEAR-PARDA-RAW}}\%) for mixed-race, {{E-YEAR-BRANCA}}\%
({{E-YEAR-BRANCA-RAW}}\%) for White and {{E-YEAR-SI}}\% ({{E-YEAR-SI-RAW}}\%)
where race is not recorded.

\begin{table}[ht]
\floatconts
  {tab:mnar}
  {\caption{Cram\'er's $V$ for CNS reliability by stratum ($n>3.9$ billion
  BI records; all $\chi^2$ $p<10^{-300}$).}}
  {\small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{4pt}}r@{\hspace{6pt}}l@{}}
  \toprule
  Dimension & $V$ & Worst-off category \\
  \midrule
  Year of care & 0.324 & 2008 (58.3\%); 2024: 5.8\% \\
  Race/colour & 0.197 & not recorded (27.7\%) \\
  State & 0.135 & Distrito Federal (22.6\%) \\
  Age band & 0.132 & under 1 year (37.0\%) \\
  Facility type & 0.130 & range 0.3\%--53.6\% \\
  Region & 0.061 & Southeast (13.9\%) \\
  Sex & 0.025 & code ``0'' (100\%) \\
  \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Collisions and Cadastral Entropy}
\runin{Chance baseline} Under a Poisson null in which each (CNS, triplet) is a
distinct person and birth dates follow the state's distribution within each
municipality $\times$ sex cell, {{F-CHANCE}}\% of occupied triplets would hold
more than one CNS, against 50.96\% observed (ratio {{F-CHANCE-RATIO}}). The null
ignores the clustering of birth dates within municipalities and predicts
{{F-TRINCAS-ESP}} million distinct triplets against {{F-TRINCAS-OBS}} million
observed, so {{F-CHANCE}}\% is a lower bound on chance collision: most of the
sharing is different people coinciding.

\runin{Entropy} For a CNS with $N$ records split across $K$ identities with
counts $n_i$, $H=-\sum_i (n_i/N)\log_2(n_i/N)$, and the Miller--Madow
correction adds $(K-1)/(2N\ln 2)$. Mean entropy is {{F-H-MM}} bits (plug-in
{{F-H-PLUGIN}}). The share of numbers with more than one identity rises from
{{MULTI-2A4}}\% (2--4 records) to {{MULTI-100}}\% (100 or more), and is nearly
flat across facility-volume deciles (18.3--24.3\%): a patient's own utilisation,
not the facility, drives drift.

\subsection{Candidate-Pair Score}
On BI$\times$AQ pairs blocked by state, only municipality and sex are shared (AQ
has no birth date), so the score takes four values. With $u$ for municipality
measured within state ({{F-UMUN-UF}}), matching the blocking, the true-match rate
is {{F-ORDER}} in the score, and the highest bucket has a true-match rate 177
times that of the lowest.

\subsection{Recall per Record, Stability and Blocking Precision}
A key is \emph{stable} for a patient if it takes the same value on all of the
patient's BI records: R1 29.66\%, R3 74.81\%, R4 62.02\% (185 million patients
with $\geq 2$ records). This is far stricter than what blocking needs. Per
record --- does a held-out record share the key with any other record of the
same patient? --- recall is {{A-R1}}\% for R1, {{A-R3}}\% for R3 and {{A-R4}}\%
for R4. In the blind BI$\times$AQ linkage, blocking recovers 97.69\% of true
pairs and the classifier (sex) keeps 99.62\% of those; a random pair sharing only
the municipality block is a true match with probability $2.07\times10^{-8}$ (one
in {{BLK-1IN}} million), which is why the classifier stage is needed.

\subsection{Completeness, Recall and FNR}
\label{app:completeness}

\begin{table}[ht]
\floatconts
  {tab:completeness}
  {\caption{Blind-linkage recall (BI$\times$AQ, $n$={{REC-N-NAC}}) by race and by
  completeness score (sex, race and region recorded).}}
  {\small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}
  \toprule
  Stratum & Recall \\
  \midrule
  Completeness 3/3 ($n$=2{,}655{,}529) & 97.9\% \\
  Completeness 2/3 ($n$=127{,}691) & 85.7\% \\
  Completeness 1/3 ($n$=407) & 0.0\% \\
  \midrule
  Race not recorded & {{REC-SI}}\% \\
  Indigenous ($n$=1{,}581) & {{REC-INDIGENA}}\% \\
  Asian descent & {{REC-AMARELA}}\% \\
  Mixed (pardo) & {{REC-PARDA}}\% \\
  White & {{REC-BRANCA}}\% \\
  Black & {{REC-PRETA}}\% \\
  \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The completeness score includes race, so comparing scores mixes the race question
with missing blocking fields: 99.3\% of the 2/3 stratum lacks race, and the few
records with race but without sex or region cannot be blocked at all. We
therefore estimate the missing-race gap directly: crude {{E-RAW}} points;
{{E-BLQ}} among records with sex and region recorded; {{E-ADJ}} from a logistic
model with year and state fixed effects (g-computation, standard errors clustered
by state); and {{E-FAC}} by Mantel--Haenszel over {{E-FAC-N}} facility $\times$
year strata containing both groups (bootstrap over facilities). The two adjusted
estimates are not nested. FNR differences with bootstrap over facilities are
{{F-CI1-CNES}} (Black/mixed vs.\ White) and {{F-CI2-CNES}} (North/Northeast vs.\
South/Southeast); intervals clustered by municipality, facility or state are
{{F-WIDEN-MIN}}--{{F-WIDEN-MAX}} times wider than patient-level ones, and all
exclude zero.

\section{Fellegi--Sunter Parameters}
\label{app:fs}

\begin{table}[ht]
\floatconts
  {tab:fs}
  {\caption{Fellegi--Sunter parameters, national BI (233{,}084{,}900 patients).
  Single-field $u$ to three significant digits.
  $u$ excludes self-pairs, $\sum n_i(n_i-1)/N(N-1)$; $m$ is measured on the joint
  key among records of the same CNS, weighted by patient.}}
  {\small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{4pt}}r@{\hspace{5pt}}r@{\hspace{5pt}}r@{}}
  \toprule
  Key & $u$ & $m$ & Bits \\
  \midrule
  Birth date + sex + mun. & $1.32\times10^{-7}$ & {{D-M}} & {{D-BITS-NAT}} \\
  Birth date & $3.29\times10^{-5}$ & 0.973 & 14.9 \\
  Municipality & $7.65\times10^{-3}$ & 0.924 & 6.9 \\
  Sex & 0.515 & 0.989 & 0.9 \\
  \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The combined $m$ is measured jointly ({{D-M}}); the product of the single-field
values would give {{D-M-PROD}}. The $u$ excludes self-pairs: a Simpson index
$\sum n_i^2/N^2$ (the chance that two draws with replacement agree) pairs each
identifier with itself, and in the nephroblastoma pool
of {{D-POOL}} identifiers that term would be {{D-SELFSHARE}}\% of the joint $u$.
Without it, the joint $u$ of birth date + sex + municipality in that pool is
{{D-RATIO-NOSELF}}$\times$ the independent product, and among pairs agreeing on
birth date the share also agreeing on sex and municipality is {{D-COND-NEW}}
(independence: {{D-COND-IND}}). Non-matches are pairs of distinct identifiers, so
a child recorded under two numbers inflates $u$: of the {{D-PAIRS}} such pairs,
{{D-DUP-CNES-PCT}}\% share a facility and {{D-DUP-RHC}} share a registry tumour
record. Excluding pairs seen at the same facility in the same month gives
{{D-RATIO}}$\times$, in the same year {{D-RATIO-ANO}}$\times$, and ever
{{D-RATIO-ALLCNES}}$\times$. Nationally the ratio is {{D-RATIO-NAT}}$\times$.
The per-pair $u$ depends on who is in the pool, not on its size: the cohort's
larger $u$ reflects a narrow range of birth dates and geographic concentration. Summing per-field
weights therefore overstates the evidence of a cohort match, and the overstatement
is specific to the cohort.

