\subsection{Propagation of Link Loss}
\label{app:propagation}
In each draw, each cascade-dependent death is removed (censored at the same time)
with the loss probability of the scenario: the national FNR of the patient's
completeness stratum, or a uniform rate. False links are added among survivors in
Binomial($|$C2$|$, $r$) draws, with $r$ = 5.7\% (share of SIM deaths claimed by
more than one patient) or 21\% (every ambiguous C2 death treated as false); the
false death time is drawn from the C2 death times and truncated at the patient's
follow-up. For the racial gap, the Black/mixed loss rate is 1.38 times the White
rate (the national ratio). Figure~\ref{fig:propagation} shows the whole range.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\floatconts
  {fig:propagation}
  {\caption{(a) 5-year OS as the cascade-dependent death links are lost (median
  and 95\% range over draws), with the three measured loss rates marked, and the
  bracket left by unrecorded and likely false registry deaths (shaded). (b) The
  White vs.\ Black/mixed survival gap under race-differential loss (Black/mixed
  rate 1.38 times the White rate): it narrows, so linkage error would understate
  the disparity.}}
  {\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/figB_propagation.pdf}}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure}[ht]
\floatconts
  {fig:km}
  {\caption{Overall survival when death sources are omitted. Every missed death
  becomes a censored patient, so survival is overstated from the first months.}}
  {\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/figC_km_scenarios.pdf}}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Capture--Recapture and the Registry-Only Deaths}

\begin{table}[ht]
\floatconts
  {tab:decomp}
  {\caption{Registry-only deaths ($n$={{RX-SORHC}}), searched in SIM by date of
  death + birth date. The same search finds {{BOTH-INSIM-PCT}}\% of the deaths
  both sources hold.}}
  {\small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.8\linewidth}r@{}}
  \toprule
  Outcome of the search & $n$ \\
  \midrule
  In SIM; our link failed on the diagnosis-code filter & {{SORHC-SEM}} \\
  In SIM; municipality of residence differs & {{SORHC-MUN}} \\
  In SIM; passes all filters as reconstructed & {{SORHC-OTHER}} \\
  Not in SIM & {{SORHC-NOTSIM}} \\
  \quad after the key's miss rate (upper bound) & {{CR-FALSOS}} \\
  \quad of all {{RX-SORHC}}: entered by the municipality route & {{MUNROUTE-SORHC}} \\
  \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The two death lists overlap: {{RX-AMBOS}} deaths in both, {{RX-SORHC}} only in
the registry, {{RX-SOPIPE}} only in the pipeline. Within five years the pipeline
also records {{A-RHC}} of the registry's deaths; this is an overlap between two
incomplete lists, not a recall. The Chapman estimator
$\hat N=(n_1+1)(n_2+1)/(m+1)-1$ with Seber's variance gives {{CR-N}}
[{{CR-N-LO}}, {{CR-N-HI}}] deaths; the pipeline captures {{CR-CAP-PIPE}}\%, the
registry {{CR-CAP-RHC}}\% and their union {{CR-CAP-REF}}\%; patients of unknown
stage are captured least ({{CR-CAP-IGN}}\%). Stratified by stage, time to death
or year, the unrecorded deaths number {{CR-MISSED-STRAT-MIN}}--{{CR-MISSED-STRAT-MAX}};
they are added among survivors of each stage in proportion to the stratified
estimate. Dependence between lists (a well-registered patient appears in both)
would make $\hat N$ too small; false links in a list make it too large.

The direct search uses no municipality and no diagnosis code; it misses deaths
whose recorded dates disagree between sources, at a rate $b$ =
{{CR-BASE-NF}}\% among the deaths both lists hold. With $F$ false deaths among
the $N$ registry-only ones, the not-found count is $F + b(N-F)$, so
$F = (\text{not found} - bN)/(1-b)$ = {{CR-FALSOS}}. This is an upper bound: $b$
is measured on deaths the link had already found, which are easier to find than
a true death it missed. Without these likely false deaths, capture--recapture
estimates {{CR-MISSED-AJ}} unrecorded deaths instead of {{CR-MISSED}}. The search key needs both dates to agree; among
children a coincidental death with the same birth and death date is negligible.

\subsection{The Registry Link}
\label{app:rhclink}
The registry link uses no birth date (the registry records age at diagnosis). It
was re-run inside SIA on {{RHC-SONDAS}} chemotherapy and radiotherapy records
(which carry a diagnosis date and the CNS) against patient identities: at the
75-day window it links {{RHC-COB}}\% of probes with precision {{RHC-PREC}}\% and
recall {{RHC-REC}}\%. Because each probe also contributes to its reference
identity, these are upper bounds. The municipality fallback is unreliable
({{RHC-MUN-OK}} of {{RHC-MUN-N}} correct). The {{RHC-MUNROUTE-N}} cohort patients
linked that way remain in the reference cohort; they account for
{{MUNROUTE-SORHC}} of the {{RX-SORHC}} registry-only deaths, and excluding them
gives OS {{RHC-CNES-OS}}\% and HR {{RHC-CNES-HR}}. Restricting to the strictest
matches (same hospital, diagnosis dates within 7 days, same estimated birth year;
$n$={{RHC-STRICT-N}}) gives OS {{RHC-STRICT-OS}}\% {{RHC-STRICT-OS-CI}} and HR
{{RHC-STRICT-HR}}; across strict strata OS ranges
{{RHC-OS-MIN}}--{{RHC-OS-MAX}}\% and HR {{RHC-HR-MIN}}--{{RHC-HR-MAX}}. The strict
OS lies above the bracket of Figure~\ref{fig:scenarios}, which is the direction
false registry deaths predict; strict matches may also select better-kept
records, so this is a subsample, not a correction.

\subsection{Race as the Missing Variable}
\label{app:race}
Of the 2{,}622 White, Black/mixed and race-missing patients, 119 lack race
(Table~\ref{tab:race}).

\begin{table}[ht]
\floatconts
  {tab:race}
  {\caption{White vs.\ Black/mixed-race patients, nephroblastoma cohort, with race
  as the missing variable. All three assume that race is missing at random given
  the covariates.}}
  {\small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.42\linewidth}rr@{}}
  \toprule
  Method & HR [95\% CI] & OS gap \\
  \midrule
  Complete case & {{F-CC}} & {{F-CC-GAP}} \\
  Inverse probability weighting & {{F-IPW}} & {{F-IPW-GAP}} \\
  Multiple imputation & {{F-MI}} & {{F-MI-GAP}} \\
  \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Complete case: HR (White vs.\ Black/mixed) {{F-CC}}, OS gap {{F-CC-GAP}} points.
Inverse probability weighting, with the probability that race is recorded
modelled on age, sex, stage, region, year, treatment group, the 5-year event and
the Nelson--Aalen cumulative hazard, stabilised by the marginal probability
(weights {{F-W-MIN}}--{{F-W-MAX}}), robust variance: {{F-IPW}}, gap
{{F-IPW-GAP}}. Multiple imputation ({{F-IMP}} imputations, logistic imputation
model including the outcome, Rubin's rules \citep{rubin1987}): {{F-MI}}, gap
{{F-MI-GAP}}. Inverse probability weighting and complete case agree because the
covariates predict missing race weakly; all three assume that race is missing at
random given the covariates.

\section{Breast-Cancer Model: Full Results}
\label{app:c50}

\begin{table}[ht]
\floatconts
  {tab:c50stage}
  {\caption{Crude mortality over follow-up (to December 2024) by stage under
  each label, breast-cancer model population.}}
  {\small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{4pt}}r@{\hspace{5pt}}r@{\hspace{5pt}}r@{}}
  \toprule
  & Pipeline & Registry & Union \\
  \midrule
  Stage I & {{U-MORT-SIM-I}}\% & {{U-MORT-RHC-I}}\% & {{U-MORT-Uniao-I}}\% \\
  Stage IV & {{U-MORT-SIM-IV}}\% & {{U-MORT-RHC-IV}}\% & {{U-MORT-Uniao-IV}}\% \\
  Ratio IV/I & {{U-RATIO-SIM}} & {{U-RATIO-RHC}} & {{U-RATIO-Uniao}} \\
  \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\runin{The pipeline label} Of its {{U-N-SIM}} deaths, {{C50-C1}}
({{C50-C1-PCT}}\%) are flags on the patient's own chemotherapy, radiotherapy or
hospital records (C1, no link, dated at the last record) and {{C50-C2}} come from
the SIM link (C2); the registry confirms {{C50-C1-CONF}}\% and {{C50-C2-CONF}}\%
of them. The stage IV/I mortality ratio is {{C50-RATIO-C1}} for C1 and
{{C50-RATIO-C2}} for C2: deaths during treatment concentrate in advanced stage,
which steepens the pipeline gradient.

\runin{Registry-only deaths} They make up {{U-SORHC-I}}\% of stage I and
{{U-SORHC-IV}}\% of stage IV patients, whereas pipeline-only deaths rise from
{{U-SOSIM-I}}\% to {{U-SOSIM-IV}}\%. Searched in SIM by date of death and birth
date, {{C50-SORHC-INSIM}} of {{C50-SORHC-N}} are found, against
{{C50-BOTH-INSIM}}\% of deaths both sources hold; the key's hit rate itself rises
with stage ({{C50-BOTH-INSIM-I}}\% in stage I, {{C50-BOTH-INSIM-IV}}\% in stage
IV). Correcting for the key's miss rate as in Appendix~\ref{app:casestudy}
(overall, and stage by stage for the split), about {{C50-FALSOS}} registry-only
deaths ({{C50-FALSOS-PCT}}\%; {{C50-FALSOS-SHARE-RHC}}\% of all
registry-labelled deaths) are not in SIM: {{C50-FALSOS-PCT-I}}\% in stage I
against {{C50-FALSOS-PCT-IV}}\% in stage IV. In adults this figure is indicative
only: the key's hit rate is measured on deaths already linked (overstating it),
and a coincidental death with the same birth and death date is not rare
(understating it); the stage pattern is the robust part. Capture--recapture on
these lists would imply a {{C50-CR-MORT}}\% mortality and is not usable here.

\runin{Calibration and injected loss} Within its own label each model is
calibrated (decile error {{C-DEC-SIM-SIM}}, {{C-DEC-RHC-RHC}} and
{{C-DEC-Uniao-Uniao}} points; Figure~\ref{fig:calib}); against the union, the
pipeline- and registry-trained models under-predict (predicted/observed
{{C-OE-SIM-Uniao}} and {{C-OE-RHC-Uniao}}). By race, against the union, the
pipeline model under-predicts White, mixed-race and Black patients by
{{C-UND-SIM-BRANCA}}, {{C-UND-SIM-PARDA}} and {{C-UND-SIM-PRETA}} points; the
registry model by {{C-UND-RHC-BRANCA}}, {{C-UND-RHC-PARDA}} and
{{C-UND-RHC-PRETA}}. Under the registry label the region HRs are extreme (South
{{C-HR-SUL-RHC}}, Southeast {{C-HR-SE-RHC}}; pipeline {{C-HR-SUL-SIM}},
{{C-HR-SE-SIM}}): registry death follow-up differs by hospital. We inject loss
into the {{C-INJ-ELIG}} deaths that exist solely through the SIM link (C2, not in
the registry). Lost at the per-race file-pair FNR (about {{C-INJ1-LOST}}, 50 draws), the stage IV HR is
{{C-INJ1-IV}} and the decile error {{C-INJ1-CAL}} points; at the cascade's loss
({{C-INJC-LOST}} lost), {{C-INJC-IV}}; at five times the measured rate,
{{C-INJ5-IV}} and {{C-INJ5-CAL}}.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\floatconts
  {fig:calib}
